NLContent regression test to assert skipped fields remain unchanged while non-skipped fields are redacted.","user":{"login":"Copilot","name":"Copilot","email":null,"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/946600?v=4","id":175728472},"id":3147501356,"pull_request_review_id":4181136702,"in_reply_to_id":null,"created_at":"2026-04-27T13:08:22Z","review":null,"side":"RIGHT","line":274,"start_line":270,"path":"redact/redact_test.go","body_html":"<p dir=\"auto\">This test currently validates collectJSONLReplacements only; it won’t catch cases where applyJSONReplacements redacts skipped fields due to value collisions (e.g., session_id and content sharing the same secret). Consider adding an end-to-end JSONLContent regression test to assert skipped fields remain unchanged while non-skipped fields are redacted.</p>"},{"url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/1045#discussion_r3147501418","body":"applyJSONReplacements falls back to strings.ReplaceAll when r.key is empty, which can accidentally redact values in fields that were intentionally skipped by shouldSkipJSONLField if the same string appears in both a scanned field and a skipped field (e.g., {\"session_id\":\"<secret>\",\"content\":\"<secret>\"} would redact session_id). Consider always scoping replacements to a JSON key when the secret originated from an object field (populate jsonReplacement.key for normal string redactions too), so skipped fields remain untouched even on value collisions.\n```suggestion\n\t\t\t// Unscoped replacements can redact identical values in fields that were\n\t\t\t// intentionally skipped during scanning, so only apply replacements when\n\t\t\t// the originating JSON object key is known.\n```","user":{"login":"Copilot","name":"Copilot","email":null,"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/946600?v=4","id":175728472},"id":3147501418,"pull_request_review_id":4181136702,"in_reply_to_id":null,"created_at":"2026-04-27T13:08:22Z","review":null,"side":"RIGHT","line":509,"start_line":null,"path":"redact/redact.go","body_html":"<p dir=\"auto\">applyJSONReplacements falls back to strings.ReplaceAll when r.key is empty, which can accidentally redact values in fields that were intentionally skipped by shouldSkipJSONLField if the same string appears in both a scanned field and a skipped field (e.g., {\"session_id\":\"\",\"content\":\"\"} would redact session_id). Consider always scoping replacements to a JSON key when the secret originated from an object field (populate jsonReplacement.key for normal string redactions too), so skipped fields remain untouched even on value collisions.</p>\n  <div class=\"my-2 border rounded-2 js-suggested-changes-blob diff-view js-check-hidden-unicode\" id=\"\">\n    <div class=\"f6 p-2 lh-condensed border-bottom d-flex\">\n      <div class=\"flex-auto flex-items-center color-fg-muted\">\n        Suggested change\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div itemprop=\"text\" class=\"blob-wrapper data file rounded-0\" style=\"margin: 0; border: none; overflow-y: visible; overflow-x: auto;\">\n      <table class=\"d-table tab-size mb-0 width-full\" data-paste-markdown-skip=\"\">\n          <tbody><tr class=\"border-0\">\n            <td class=\"blob-num blob-num-deletion text-right border-0 px-2 py-1 lh-default\" data-line-number=\"509\"></td>\n            <td class=\"border-0 px-2 py-1 blob-code-inner blob-code-deletion js-blob-code-deletion blob-code-marker-deletion\">\t\t\t<span class=\"pl-s1 x x-first\">s</span><span class=\"x\"> </span><span class=\"pl-c1 x\">=</span><span class=\"x\"> </span><span class=\"pl-s1 x\">strings</span><span class=\"x\">.</span><span class=\"pl-c1 x\">ReplaceAll</span><span class=\"x\">(</span><span class=\"pl-s1 x\">s</span><span class=\"x\">, </span><span class=\"pl-s1 x\">origJSON</span><span class=\"x\">, </span><span class=\"pl-s1 x\">replJSON</span><span class=\"x x-last\">)</span></td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr class=\"border-0\">\n            <td class=\"blob-num blob-