{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T14:45:50.336Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Yes, that’s the distinction.\n\nThere are three “places” here:\n\n1. Raw agent transcript on disk: Entire generally does not rewrite this. It can contain raw secrets because it is the agent’s own log file.\n\n2. Checkpoint transcript persisted by Entire: this is where `redact.JSONLContent` runs before writing `full.jsonl` to `entire/checkpoints/v1`. This is what entire.io should be reading/displaying for checkpoint history.\n\n3. A string value inside a JSONL transcript record: the redactor parses each JSONL line and scans individual string fields.\n\nThe evidence I found:\n\n- A full key block inside one string was redacted:\n  `key:\\nREDACTED`, another had one payload line, another had `-----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----`.\n\nThat means it is not “SSH keys never redact.” It is “SSH keys redact when the detector sees the full block, but fragmented transcript records can bypass the full-block rule.”\n\nI’d call it an integration blind spot more than purely a Betterleaks bug. Betterleaks’ default private-key rule is designed to match a complete private-key block. Entire’s JSONL redactor intentionally scans string values field-by-field to avoid over-redacting IDs/paths, but that means multi-line streamed output may be split before Betterleaks sees the whole block.\n\nSo if the leaked key appeared in the persisted Entire checkpoint transcript in that chunked form, entire.io would also show it unredacted before this fix. If you mean the original local agent transcript file, that is expected to be raw and is not the security boundary.","phase":"commentary","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T14:45:50.337Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes, that’s the distinction.\n\nThere are three “places” here:\n\n1. Raw agent transcript on disk: Entire generally does not rewrite this. It can contain raw secrets because it is the agent’s own log file.\n\n2. Checkpoint transcript persisted by Entire: this is where `redact.JSONLContent` runs before writing `full.jsonl` to `entire/checkpoints/v1`. This is what entire.io should be reading/displaying for checkpoint history.\n\n3. A string value inside a JSONL transcript record: the redactor parses each JSONL line and scans individual string fields.\n\nThe evidence I found:\n\n- A full key block inside one string was redacted:\n  `key:\\nREDACTED`, another had one payload line, another had `-----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----`.\n\nThat means it is not “SSH keys never redact.” It is “SSH keys redact when the detector sees the full block, but fragmented transcript records can bypass the full-block rule.”\n\nI’d call it an integration blind spot more than purely a Betterleaks bug. Betterleaks’ default private-key rule is designed to match a complete private-key block. Entire’s JSONL redactor intentionally scans string values field-by-field to avoid over-redacting IDs/paths, but that means multi-line streamed output may be split before Betterleaks sees the whole block.\n\nSo if the leaked key appeared in the persisted Entire checkpoint transcript in that chunked form, entire.io would also show it unredacted before this fix. If you mean the original local agent transcript file, that is expected to be raw and is not the security boundary."}],"phase":"commentary"}}
