{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T15:01:37.854Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"“Opaque” just means the value has no recognizable format from the redactor’s point of view.\n\nA recognizable secret has structure, for example:\n\n```text\n-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----\n```\n\nor:\n\n```text\ngithub_pat_...\nsk-...\nxoxb-...\nGITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=...\n```\n\nThose give detectors clues: markers, prefixes, key names, assignment syntax, provider-specific shapes.\n\nAn opaque value is more like:\n\n```text\na0f91c8d7e3b...\n```\n\nor:\n\n```text\na0AbCd123...\n```\n\nStanding alone, that could be:\n- a secret token\n- a container ID\n- a session ID\n- a commit-ish hash\n- a random internal identifier\n- a slug generated by Wrangler\n\nThe redactor has to decide from the string alone. If it redacts every `a0...` value, it will also redact lots of harmless IDs and make transcripts much less useful. So generic opaque values usually only get redacted if they are high entropy enough or appear next to a sensitive key name/pattern.\n\nIn your case:\n\n```sh\nnpx wrangler containers ssh a0...(rest of key)\n```\n\nthe important context is not inside the value itself; it is the command context. A generic detector sees only “some opaque argument.” A Wrangler-aware detector could say: “argument after `wrangler containers ssh` is sensitive in our logs, redact it.”\n\nSo “opaque” means: secret-like to a human with context, but not self-identifying to a regex/entropy detector.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
