{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T15:02:13.019Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Entropy is about character variety and unpredictability, not just length.\n\nA long hex-ish string like:\n\n```text\na0f91c8d7e3b...\n```\n\nuses only 16 possible characters: `0-9a-f`. Its Shannon entropy per character is capped at 4 bits, even if it is truly random hex. Entire’s current generic threshold is `> 4.5`, so pure hex often won’t pass.\n\nExample:\n\n- Random base64 can approach ~6 bits/char.\n- Random alphanumeric can approach ~5.95 bits/char.\n- Random hex maxes at 4 bits/char.\n- A UUID is often lower because of dashes and version bits.\n- Repeated or patterned hex is lower still.\n\nSo a very long hex token can be genuinely sensitive and still miss a `4.5` entropy threshold. That’s why secret scanners usually combine entropy with context/prefix rules. For `npx wrangler containers ssh a0f91...`, the fix should probably be context-aware: redact the sensitive argument after that command, not lower the global entropy threshold and start redacting harmless hashes everywhere.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
