{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T15:07:47.190Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Yes, transcript-focused is the right framing.\n\nBetterleaks is mostly a **secret/token scanner**, not an account-number/privacy scanner. It is good at things like known token prefixes, key/value credentials, and full private-key blocks. It generally does **not** broadly redact account numbers, app IDs, client IDs, container IDs, or publisher IDs, because those look too much like normal identifiers.\n\n`GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET` is different from an account number. Semantically it is a secret, but whether Betterleaks catches it depends on the transcript shape:\n\n```sh\nREDACTED\n```\n\nLikely to be caught by generic credential or entropy rules.\n\n```sh\nprintf 'REDACTED' | npx wrangler secret put GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET\n```\n\nMuch easier to miss, because the value and the key name are separated by shell syntax. The scanner may just see an opaque `printf` argument, not “this is the GitHub client secret.”\n\nFor things like `GOOGLE_ADSENSE_ACCOUNT`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `Application ID`: those are usually identifiers, not secrets. Betterleaks probably will not consistently redact them. But in transcripts, if they appear in a `wrangler secret put ...` command, we should treat them as sensitive because the user/tool is explicitly storing them as a secret.\n\nSo the transcript-specific gap is: **we need command-aware redaction**, especially for forms like:\n\n```sh\nprintf '<value>' | npx wrangler secret put <NAME>\nnpx wrangler containers ssh <opaque-arg>\n```\n\nThat is separate from Betterleaks’ normal file-style secret detection.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
