{"timestamp":"2026-05-13T15:20:56.371Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Agreed. We should not add a broad “after any `ssh` redact the next long string” rule.\n\nA safer policy is:\n\n- Keep generic entropy threshold as-is.\n- Keep Betterleaks for known secret shapes.\n- Add narrow transcript-aware tests for the exact observed patterns.\n- Only add rules when the surrounding context is unambiguously secret-handling, not just “SSH appears somewhere.”\n\nFor Wrangler, `npx wrangler secret put NAME` is unambiguous: stdin/value is being stored as a secret, so redacting the piped `printf` value is reasonable.\n\nFor `npx wrangler containers ssh <arg>`, I’d first verify the command semantics. If `<arg>` is a container/session identifier, blanket redaction may be overkill. If it is key material or an SSH credential, then we should redact only the sensitive-looking argument under that exact command shape, with guardrails.\n\nGood guardrails:\n- Require `wrangler containers ssh` in the same string.\n- Redact only a long non-flag positional argument, not command names/options.\n- Do not redact ordinary hostnames, paths, or short IDs.\n- Add “should not redact” tests for harmless commands/IDs if we can identify them.\n- Prefer redacting values in persisted transcripts, not rewriting raw local agent logs.\n\nI’d treat this as a targeted transcript-redaction rule after confirming what Wrangler expects there.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
